# models_list.yaml: the catalog of models Certo can choose from, with
# their ratings. No keys, no addresses: those are personal and go in
# models.yaml. The task profiles (--auto-model) are in task_profiles.yaml,
# and the escalation ladders (--escalate) in escalation.yaml; both are
# optional.
#
# Certo ships this file only as an example and never reads the example.
# Copy it to models_list.yaml (in your project folder, or in
# ~/.config/certo/) and edit it: add models, change ratings. A new Certo
# release updates this example, never your copy.
#
# Read by --auto-model, --escalate, certo bench and certo check, together
# with models.yaml. With the default (manual) model selection, no model
# file is read. See docs/reference/models-yaml.md,
# docs/guides/pick-models-automatically.md and
# docs/guides/escalate-between-models.md.
#
# =============================================================================
# HOW RATINGS/WEIGHTS WORK — read this before scoring your own models
# =============================================================================
#
# Each model gets a `ratings` map (numbers 1-5; `cost` has one decimal,
# worked out from the price, see below). Each task profile
# (in task_profiles.yaml) gets a `weights` map over the same axis names. For
# auto-selection a model's score for a task is:
#
#     score = sum( rating[axis] * weight[axis] for axis in weights )
#
# computed for every active model, then sorted descending into a ranked
# fallback chain — see rank_candidates()/score_entry() in model_catalog.py.
#
# `--escalate` without an explicit ladder builds a "derived" ladder from
# capability = reasoning + precision + code, climbing from the least to the
# most capable model; `cost` is only used to break ties there.
#
# CONVENTION — on every axis, HIGHER = MORE DESIRABLE. This includes `cost`:
#   reasoning / precision / code   5 = excellent, 1 = weak
#   cost                           5 = cheapest (local = free), 1 = most expensive
#                                   (NOT "how much it costs" — the opposite!)
#
# Why `cost` is inverted: every weight in this file is positive and scoring
# is a plain weighted sum, so a positive weight always rewards a HIGHER
# rating. Rating cost as cost-*effectiveness* keeps every axis on the same
# arithmetic. If you add a custom axis, rate it the same way.
#
# Axis names are open-ended — add your own (e.g. `latency`) with zero code
# changes. A model that doesn't rate itself on an axis a profile weights
# contributes 0 for that axis, not an error.
#
# =============================================================================
# RATING SCALE — the anchors behind the numbers in this file
# =============================================================================
#
# The three capability axes are meant to be comparable ACROSS providers:
# a 4 in `code` means the same thing for every model. 5 is the frontier as
# of the date below (Claude Opus 5.5 / Fable 5.1, GPT-6 Astra, Gemini 4
# Argon). When a new frontier ships, everything else may need to move down.
#
#   code       5  Terminal-Bench 4.0 >= ~55%  or  SWE-Bench Pro >= ~80%
#              4  Terminal-Bench 4.0 ~35-54%  or  SWE-Bench Pro ~64-79%
#              3  Terminal-Bench 4.0 ~15-34%  or  Terminal-Bench 2.1 >= ~80%
#                 with SWE-Bench Pro ~58-63%
#              2  SWE-Bench Pro ~45-57%  or  SWE-bench Verified ~65-80%
#                 or  Terminal-Bench 2.0 ~45-65%
#              1  below that (SWE-bench Verified < ~60%), or no reliable
#                 multi-step agent loop
#   reasoning  5  top of the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index plus
#                 FrontierMath Tier 4 >= ~85% / HLE (tools) >= ~60%
#              4  AA Index just below the top, FrontierMath Tier 4 ~65-84%
#                 (AA Index values here are on its v5.0.1 scale, Nov 2026;
#                 v5.0 changed the scale, so older values don't compare)
#              3  GPQA Diamond ~88-93%, weaker on frontier maths
#              2  GPQA Diamond ~75-87%
#              1  below that, or not measured on hard reasoning
#   precision  No benchmark covers every provider. Proxies: hallucination /
#              factuality evals, long-context retrieval (MRCR), tool-use
#              evals (tau2, Toolathlon), instruction following (IFBench).
#              The least certain axis everywhere — treat +/-1 as noise.
#   cost       From the blended price p per 1M tokens at a 3:1
#              input:output mix, standard tier, list price (no batch, no
#              cache):  cost = 4 - log2(p) / 2, one decimal, kept between
#              1 and 4.9; 5 only for local/free. Each doubling of the price
#              takes half a point: $0.20 -> 4.9, $1 -> 4.0, $4 -> 3.0,
#              $8 -> 2.5, $20 -> 1.8. The price rated is the first
#              "$X in / $Y out" in the model's description;
#              tests/test_model_catalog.py checks every cloud model's cost
#              against it, so change both together.
#
# Benchmarks are vendor-reported unless a description says otherwise, and
# were run at high/xhigh/max reasoning effort — which is why the cloud
# reasoning models below set `reasoning_effort: "high"`. At lower effort,
# expect less than the ratings say.
#
# Local models are rated as they run here: 4-bit quantized via Ollama with
# a ~32K context window. Published scores used 128K+ context and 100+ agent
# turns, so the local ratings are deliberately conservative. Make sure the
# Ollama server really uses a large enough window (e.g. start it with
# OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=32768); otherwise prompts are silently truncated.
#
# Prices, model ids and benchmark numbers checked on 2026-11-07. They
# change often — re-check provider pricing pages and LiteLLM's docs before
# relying on one (see CLAUDE.md's "Model ID strings ... change over time").
#
# Which models Certo may use is decided in models.yaml, not here: a model
# listed here but not in models.yaml is never used. models.yaml gives each
# chosen model, by `name`, its api_key or base_url, `activated`, and any
# overrides of the fields below.

models:
  # ===========================================================================
  # LOCAL / SELF-HOSTED, OPEN-WEIGHT — served by Ollama (port 11434)
  #
  # Free to run, so cost: 5 for all of them. Tags checked against
  # ollama.com/library on 2026-11-07. Listed weakest-first.
  # ===========================================================================

  - name: ollama-local
    model: ollama/llama3
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      Meta Llama 3 8B, 8K context. Ollama gives it no tool calling, so it
      can't run Certo's agent at all: kept only as an example of an entry.
      No current agentic-coding benchmark; rated by its size and age.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 1, code: 1, cost: 5}

  - name: qwen3-local
    model: ollama/qwen3
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      Alibaba Qwen3, untagged pull = the 8B variant (~5GB, 40K context,
      tools, hybrid think/non-think). Small general model; no agentic-coding score found,
      rated as an estimate. Superseded locally by qwen3.6 below.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 1, code: 1, cost: 5}

  - name: gpt-oss-20b-local
    model: ollama/gpt-oss:20b
    reasoning_effort: ""             # always reasons; supports low / medium / high
    timeout_seconds: 600
    description: >
      OpenAI gpt-oss-20b (21B total / 3.6B active MoE, MXFP4, 13-14GB, 128K
      context, tool calling; August 2025). Fast and light, fits in 16GB.
      SWE-bench Verified 60.7%, Aider Polyglot 34.2%, GPQA Diamond 71.5%,
      tau-bench retail 54.8% at high effort (OpenAI model card; at medium
      effort 53.2%, 26.6% and 66.0%). Good for
      quick, small edits; below the 2026 local coders on multi-step work.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 2, code: 1, cost: 5}

  - name: glm-4.7-flash-local
    model: ollama/glm-4.7-flash
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 900
    description: >
      Zhipu GLM-4.7-Flash (30B total / 3B active MoE, ~19GB at Q4_K_M,
      198K context, tools and thinking; needs Ollama 0.14.3+).
      General-purpose, not code-specialized. Use the plain `glm-4.7-flash`
      tag, not `glm-4.7:cloud` (a 357B cloud model). SWE-bench Verified
      59.2%, tau2 79.5%, GPQA Diamond 75.2%, LiveCodeBench v6 64.0%
      (Zhipu model card).
    ratings: {reasoning: 2, precision: 1, code: 1, cost: 5}

  - name: qwen3-coder-local
    model: ollama/qwen3-coder:30b
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 900             # a local model on a slow machine can need more than the default 300
    description: >
      Alibaba Qwen3-Coder 30B-A3B (30B total / 3.3B active MoE, 19GB,
      256K native context; July 2025). SWE-bench Verified 51.9% in an
      independent OpenHands run (Nebius) with 128K context and 100+ turns; at 32K and Q4
      it will do worse on long agent loops. Non-thinking model, no GPQA
      score found. Fast thanks to 3.3B active params. Superseded by
      qwen3.6-35b-local on every published coding benchmark. Measured in
      Certo's 0.1.18 local benchmark: 18/27 hidden tests, fooled Certo 3
      times; qwen3.8-27b-local did better in the same run.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 2, code: 1, cost: 5}

  - name: gemma4-31b-local           # was `gemma3-local` (pointed at the 4B Gemma 3) — rename references in your ladders
    model: ollama/gemma4:31b
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 900             # 31B dense: much slower per token than the 3B-active MoE models
    description: >
      Google Gemma 4 31B dense (19-20GB, 256K context, thinking mode;
      needs a recent Ollama). Strong reasoner for its size: GPQA Diamond
      84.3%, LiveCodeBench v6 80.0%, tau2 76.9%, MRCR 128K 66.4% (Google
      model card, thinking on). Google publishes no SWE-bench score; its
      tech report gives Terminal Bench Hard 36.0%. Measured in Certo's
      0.1.15 baseline (the QAT tag below): 26/27 hidden tests, 27/27
      verified, median 12m 40s per task — the best local result so far.
      Thinking traces eat a 32K window quickly.
    ratings: {reasoning: 2, precision: 2, code: 2, cost: 5}

  - name: gemma4-31b-qat-local
    model: ollama/gemma4:31b-it-qat
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 900             # same dense 31B as gemma4-31b-local
    description: >
      The same Gemma 4 31B, in Google's quantization-aware-trained 4-bit
      version (19GB, 256K context). Trained to run at 4 bits, so it stays
      closer to the full model than an ordinary 4-bit conversion: in
      Unsloth's measurement of the equivalent GGUF, its top token agrees
      with the reference 96.7% of the time, against 87.9% for a plain Q4_0.
      Same benchmarks and limits as gemma4-31b-local; same ratings, since
      the published scores are already full precision. Prefer it to the
      plain tag.
    ratings: {reasoning: 2, precision: 2, code: 2, cost: 5}

  - name: devstral-small-2-local
    model: ollama/devstral-small-2:24b
    reasoning_effort: ""             # no thinking mode: leave empty
    timeout_seconds: 900
    description: >
      Mistral Devstral Small 2 (24B dense, 15GB, 384K context, tools and
      vision, Apache 2.0; late 2025; needs Ollama 0.13.3+). Built as a coding
      agent: SWE-bench Verified 65.8%, Terminal-Bench 32.0% (Mistral, as
      listed by Ollama); an independent leaderboard measured 56.4%. No
      reasoning benchmark published. The non-Qwen local option to compare
      against qwen3.6 and qwen3.8 in `certo bench`.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 2, code: 2, cost: 5}

  - name: qwen3.6-35b-local
    model: ollama/qwen3.6:35b-a3b
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 900
    description: >
      Alibaba Qwen3.6-35B-A3B (35B total / 3B active MoE, ~23-24GB at
      4-bit, hybrid thinking, April 2026). Best fast (3B-active) local coding
      agent in this file: SWE-bench Verified 73.4%, SWE-Bench Pro 49.5%, Terminal-Bench
      2.0 51.5%, LiveCodeBench v6 80.4% (official model card). OpenHands
      recommends it as the first local model to try and an Ollama context
      of at least ~22K. Some community GGUFs don't load in Ollama — use the
      official `qwen3.6:35b-a3b` tag (a `qwen3.6:35b-a3b-coding` tag now
      exists too, not measured here). In Certo's 0.1.15 baseline it wrote
      its tool calls as text and passed 0/27: measure before trusting it.
    ratings: {reasoning: 2, precision: 2, code: 2, cost: 5}

  - name: nemotron-3.5-lightning-local
    model: ollama/nemotron-3.5-lightning:30b
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 900
    description: >
      NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Lightning (30B total / 3B active, hybrid Mamba-2
      and MoE, 19-25GB, up to 1M context, tools and thinking). Built for
      speed (NVIDIA claims 4x throughput), not quality: SWE-bench Verified
      51.6%, Terminal-Bench 2.1 24.6%, GPQA Diamond 75.4%, IFBench 71.9%
      (NVIDIA model card). On NVIDIA's own table Qwen3.6-35B-A3B beats it on
      every coding row.
    ratings: {reasoning: 2, precision: 2, code: 1, cost: 5}

  - name: flint-40b-local
    model: ollama/flint:40b
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 900
    description: >
      Recursive Flint (39.8B total / ~2.5B active MoE, 23GB, 256K context,
      tools and thinking, Apache 2.0; autumn 2026). Vendor-reported only:
      SWE-bench Verified 74.2%, Terminal-Bench 2.1 54.9%. No independent
      result yet; tight on a 32GB machine. Measure with `certo bench`
      before trusting it.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 2, code: 2, cost: 5}

  - name: qwen3.8-27b-local
    model: ollama/qwen3.8:27b
    reasoning_effort: ""             # thinks by default (model default: xhigh); set low/medium to save context
    timeout_seconds: 1200            # dense 27B that thinks at length: slower per answer than the 3B-active MoEs
    description: >
      Alibaba Qwen3.8-27B dense (18GB, 256K context, text and image, thinking
      on by default; August 2026). Strongest local model in this file on
      paper: SWE-Bench Pro 61.7%, Terminal-Bench 2.1 73.0%, DeepSWE v1.1
      42.2%, GPQA Diamond 89.2%, IFBench 79.5%, LiveCodeBench v6 90.3%
      (official model card, full precision). Those numbers would earn code
      3; it is rated 2 because at 4-bit with a 32K window its long thinking
      traces leave little room for the task. Give it a bigger window if your
      machine allows, and measure with `certo bench`. Measured in Certo's
      0.1.18 local benchmark, with a 131K window: 27/27 hidden tests, never
      fooled Certo, median 7m 37s per task. The `certo init` default.
    ratings: {reasoning: 3, precision: 3, code: 2, cost: 5}

  # ===========================================================================
  # OPENAI — CURRENT (GPT-6 family)
  # ===========================================================================

  - name: gpt-6-luna
    model: openai/gpt-6-luna
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      OpenAI's cheapest current tier: $0.10 in / $0.50 out per 1M (Sept
      2026, half of GPT-5.6 Luna). DeepSWE v1.1 66.6% at max effort;
      Terminal-Bench 4.0 16.4% (public leaderboard). OpenAI says its
      factuality at high effort matches GPT-5.6 Sol and its alignment evals
      improve on GPT-5.6 Luna; it still failed to disclose a broken tool in
      28.7% of an adversarial eval (GPT-6 Sol: 4.9%). Same coding level as
      GPT-5.6 Luna at half the price — replaces it. Measured in Certo's
      0.1.15 baseline: 8/9 hidden tests, fooled Certo once; it built the
      Sky Hopper sample verified, 4/4 milestones, for $0.13 (2026-11-07).
    ratings: {reasoning: 3, precision: 4, code: 3, cost: 4.9}

  - name: gpt-6.1-sol
    model: openai/gpt-6.1-sol
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 600
    description: >
      Balanced tier: $2 in / $10 out per 1M (cached input $0.10).
      Terminal-Bench 4.0 58.2% (public leaderboard, max effort), level with
      GPT-6 Astra (58.2% there). OpenAI reports it matches Astra on DeepSWE
      v1.1 and comes within ~2 points of it on OSWorld 2.0, at about
      one-fifth of Astra's price. AA Intelligence Index 55 (max). Replaces
      GPT-6 Sol (same price, Terminal-Bench 4.0 49.4%), which is left out
      of this catalog.
    ratings: {reasoning: 4, precision: 5, code: 5, cost: 3.0}

  - name: gpt-6.1-terra
    model: openai/gpt-6.1-terra
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 600
    description: >
      Mid tier, October 2026: $1 in / $5 out per 1M (cached input $0.05),
      1.05M context. Terminal-Bench 4.0 50.3% at max effort (vendor; not on
      the leaderboard yet). No DeepSWE, SWE-Bench Pro or GPQA number found,
      so reasoning and precision are estimates. Replaces GPT-5.6 Terra
      (Terminal-Bench 4.0 21.5%) at half the price.
    ratings: {reasoning: 3, precision: 3, code: 4, cost: 3.5}

  - name: gpt-6-astra
    model: openai/gpt-6-astra
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 600
    description: >
      OpenAI flagship: $10 in / $50 out per 1M. Terminal-Bench 4.0 57.9%,
      DeepSWE v1.1 74.1%, GPQA Diamond 96.0%, FrontierMath Tier 4 97.6%,
      MRCR 256K-512K 100%, internal hallucination rate 4.2% vs 12.2% for
      GPT-5.6 Sol (OpenAI launch table); 58.2% on the public Terminal-Bench
      4.0 leaderboard. GPT-6.1 Sol now codes as well at a fifth of the
      price. Use when correctness matters more than cost.
    ratings: {reasoning: 5, precision: 5, code: 5, cost: 1.8}

  # ===========================================================================
  # OPENAI — PREVIOUS GENERATIONS (kept for fallback / comparison)
  #
  # All of these are dominated by a GPT-6 model that is cheaper and at least
  # as capable; leave them out of models.yaml unless you have a reason.
  # ===========================================================================

  - name: gpt-4.1-mini
    model: openai/gpt-4.1-mini
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      Legacy non-reasoning small model: $0.40 in / $1.60 out per 1M.
      Superseded by gpt-6-luna, which is cheaper and far stronger.
      Rated as an estimate; no current agentic benchmark found. OpenAI
      retires it on 14 Jan 2027.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 1, code: 1, cost: 4.3}

  - name: gpt-4.1
    model: openai/gpt-4.1
    reasoning_effort: ""
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      Legacy non-reasoning model: $2 in / $8 out per 1M. SWE-bench
      Verified 54.6%. Good at plain instruction following, weak as an
      agent. Superseded by gpt-6-luna on capability and price. OpenAI
      retires it on 14 Jan 2027.
    ratings: {reasoning: 1, precision: 2, code: 1, cost: 3.1}

  - name: gpt-5.4-mini
    model: openai/gpt-5.4-mini
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      $0.75 in / $4.50 out per 1M. SWE-Bench Pro 54.4%, Terminal-Bench 2.0
      60.0%, GPQA Diamond 88.0% (xhigh effort); MRCR 128K-256K 33.6%, so it
      loses track in long contexts. Costs more than gpt-6-luna and
      gpt-5.6-luna while scoring lower — a poor escalation rung.
    ratings: {reasoning: 3, precision: 2, code: 2, cost: 3.6}

  - name: gpt-5.4
    model: openai/gpt-5.4
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      $2.50 in / $15 out per 1M. SWE-Bench Pro 57.7%, Terminal-Bench 2.0
      75.1%, GPQA Diamond 93.0%, MRCR 128K-256K 79.3%. Superseded by
      gpt-6.1-sol, which is cheaper.
    ratings: {reasoning: 3, precision: 3, code: 2, cost: 2.8}

  - name: gpt-5.6-luna
    model: openai/gpt-5.6-luna
    reasoning_effort: "high"           # was "medium"; benchmarks below are at max effort
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      $0.20 in / $1.20 out per 1M since the 30 July 2026 cut. SWE-Bench Pro
      62.7%, Terminal-Bench 2.1 84.7%, Terminal-Bench 4.0 17.3%, GPQA
      92.3%, FrontierMath Tier 4 58.5%; MRCR 256K-512K only 41.3% (OpenAI
      table), but at 256K GraphWalks BFS 81.3% beats GPT-5.5's 73.7%, so
      precision holds for prompts of normal size. Codes as well as GPT-5.5
      for a fraction of the price, but gpt-6-luna now does the same at half
      its price.
    ratings: {reasoning: 3, precision: 4, code: 3, cost: 4.6}

  - name: gpt-5.5
    model: openai/gpt-5.5
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      $5 in / $30 out per 1M. Terminal-Bench 2.0 82.7%, SWE-Bench Pro
      58.6-59.4%, GPQA 93.6%, FrontierMath Tier 4 72.5%, MRCR 256K-512K
      81.5%. Better than GPT-5.6 Luna on hard maths and long context, no
      better at coding, at about 25x the price.
    ratings: {reasoning: 4, precision: 4, code: 3, cost: 2.3}

  - name: gpt-5.6-terra
    model: openai/gpt-5.6-terra
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      $2 in / $12 out per 1M after the July 2026 cut. SWE-Bench Pro 63.4%,
      Terminal-Bench 2.1 87.4%, FrontierMath Tier 4 68.3%, MRCR 256K-512K
      89.6% (OpenAI table); Terminal-Bench 4.0 21.5% (public leaderboard).
      Superseded by gpt-6.1-terra at half the price.
    ratings: {reasoning: 4, precision: 4, code: 3, cost: 2.9}

  - name: gpt-5.6-sol
    model: openai/gpt-5.6-sol
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 600
    description: >
      Standard list price $5 in / $30 out per 1M; $4 / $20 on a promotion
      until 30 Nov 2026 (OpenAI pricing page). SWE-Bench Pro 64.6%,
      Terminal-Bench 2.1 88.8%, Terminal-Bench 4.0 37.3%, FrontierMath
      Tier 4 83.0%. Superseded by gpt-6.1-sol, which is cheaper even at
      the promotional price, and stronger.
    ratings: {reasoning: 4, precision: 4, code: 4, cost: 2.3}

  # ===========================================================================
  # ANTHROPIC — CURRENT (Claude 5.5 family + Fable 5.1)
  # ===========================================================================

  - name: claude-haiku-5-5
    model: anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5
    reasoning_effort: "high"           # API default is medium; benchmarks below are at max
    timeout_seconds: 300
    description: >
      $0.10 in / $0.50 out per 1M for prompts up to 100K tokens; 5x that
      ($0.50 / $2.50) above 100K. Terminal-Bench 4.0 39.2%, SWE-Bench Pro
      64.8% (Anthropic, max effort); AA Intelligence Index 36. Strongest
      coder per dollar in this file, and more than its benchmarks suggest
      in Certo's own runs: every hidden test of the 0.1.15 baseline, and
      all three milestone samples (a platform game, a 3D game, a web app
      with a database) built verified with no help from a bigger model
      (2026-11-07). Weaker at hard reasoning. Watch the long-prompt price
      step if Certo sends big contexts.
    ratings: {reasoning: 3, precision: 3, code: 4, cost: 4.9}

  - name: claude-sonnet-5-5
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 600
    description: >
      $2 in / $10 out per 1M. Terminal-Bench 4.0 70.6% (64% in Artificial
      Analysis' own run — above Opus 5.5 in both), SWE-Bench Pro 81.3%,
      AA Intelligence Index 55. Frontier-level coder at mid-tier price;
      Opus 5.5 still leads on hard reasoning and most other evals.
    ratings: {reasoning: 4, precision: 4, code: 5, cost: 3.0}

  - name: claude-opus-5-5
    model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5
    reasoning_effort: "high"           # API default is medium
    timeout_seconds: 600
    description: >
      $4 in / $20 out per 1M. Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index 59 at
      max effort (57 xhigh, 52 high), second only to Gemini 4 Carbon (60).
      Terminal-Bench 4.0 66.4% at xhigh, 64.8% at max (about 60% in AA's
      run), SWE-Bench Pro 89.9%. Anthropic's recommended default ("start
      with Claude Opus 5.5 for most workloads"); cheaper than Opus 5 and
      better on every shared benchmark.
    ratings: {reasoning: 5, precision: 5, code: 5, cost: 2.5}

  - name: claude-fable-5-1
    model: anthropic/claude-fable-5-1
    reasoning_effort: "high"
    timeout_seconds: 600
